WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
Good morning, John!

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.300
Trilobites are super weird, and I want you to know more about them.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:13.300
It's easy to forget that the moment we live in is just this 
one snapshot of this huge history of life on Earth.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:16.770
And yeah, we have to, and should, and do study now...

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:22.319
But also, there's a lot to learn if we look at the past 
and it can just be fascinating, as well.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:25.979
Which is why we've teamed up with PBS Digital Studios 
to make a new show.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.479
It's called EONS and it's about the entire history of life on Earth

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:35.200
So like most biology, as what's happening right now, EONS is everything else.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.600
All of the life that has happened so far until now.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:42.700
Turns out, there's lots of good stuff to cover here.
Our first episode is about trilobites, of course.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:46.000
The emergence of trilobites happened around 500 million-ish years ago.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.879
It was part of what's called the Cambrian Explosion,
where life on Earth got way more complex.

00:00:50.879 --> 00:00:52.700
They were the first true arthropods.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:58.200
Arthropods now take up three-quarters of the world's 
species, so basically, good on you, trilobites!

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:03.300
Arthropods, basically, are the segmented exoskeleton-
covered things that you see all around you right now.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:08.000
Spiders, butterflies, millipedes, crabs, 
like those things, too.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.000
Even though horseshoe crabs share some family resemblance with trilobites

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:16.600
and some horseshoe crab species did coexist with 
trilobites, they are old horseshoe crabs.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:20.780
The last species of trilobites went extinct 
around 250 million years ago.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:23.920
But even though they did not last forever, 
they were extremely successful!

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.700
They existed for 270 million years.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:30.200
Primates, for comparison, have existed 
for around 50 million years.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:31.800
So, we have a lot of catching up to do.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:36.800
We found trilobite fossils on every continent 
and described over 15,000 species.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:41.200
One of the weird reasons why trilobites are so well
studied and why we find so many of them,

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:44.549
is that most trilobite fossils aren't actually trilobites.

00:01:44.549 --> 00:01:49.589
It's nice being an organism whose skin grows with them, 
but that is not the case for arthropods.

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:52.320
If the arthropod wants to grow, 
it has to shed its exoskeleton

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:55.900
and then move out of that old house that was too small for it.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:02:00.680
And when it does that it leaves behind that shed 
exoskeleton, which can then fossilize.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.360
and that happened a lot, obviously, with trilobites.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:08.000
So the majority of Trilobite fossils aren't the organism 
that died. They're the shed exoskeleton

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.300
which, of course, we can still use to date the trilobite,

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:13.300
and to figure out what kind it was and learn more about the species.

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:15.900
There are a bunch of reasons trilobite
were probably so successful.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.390
Their strong exoskeletons, their jointed legs.

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:22.100
But they also - this is a pretty big deal -
 had the first complex eyes.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:25.100
And trilobite eyes are freakin' awesome!

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:29.729
Some of them have thousands of individual lenses 
and the lenses are made of calcite.

00:02:29.729 --> 00:02:33.420
They're a crystal. 
The trilobite grew crystals in its eyes!

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:38.300
Our eye lenses, they can flex and change size, 
so if I look at something close-up,

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:40.630
I focus on it and then I look at something far away,
I focus on that,

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:46.800
The lenses in my eyes are actually changing shape.
If your lens is made of rock, that's hard to do.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:51.200
You  also end up with a problem that astronomers had 
in the 17th century which is that you get weird

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:55.000
distortions unless you use very specific lens shapes.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:59.310
In the 1600s, two astronomers came up with two different lens shapes that helped to fix this problem.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:01.500
Here's the one that Christiaan Huygens came up with.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:04.650
and here's the one that René Descartes came up with. 
Yes, that René Descartes.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:05.800
He did a lot of stuff.

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:11.000
And here are the eye-lens shapes for the trilobite, Crozonapsis and the trilobite Dalmanitina.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:13.900
This is so dang cool, right?

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:17.900
These lens shapes allowed for objects to be in focus 
when they were pretty close up or pretty far away

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:20.200
and also got rid of some of that lens distortion.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:25.200
It took humans 300 million years 
to catch up with trilobite evolution!

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:25.830
I just love it

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.380
And if you want to learn more about trilobites,
our first episode is up!

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:35.130
youtube.com/eons, where me, Blake and Callie will be taking you through the entire history of life on Earth!

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:38.760
It's going to be fun! Join us there. 
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

